\paragraph{Scope and source qualification.}The source calls a and its adjoint raising and lowering in the opposite order to the standard convention. The displayed Hamiltonian is retained and the convention is made explicit. No positivity, nonzero-parameter restriction, or compact-resolvent claim is inserted. The catalogue title/status was paired with a machine-generated research attempt; that attempt is not peer-reviewed evidence of settlement. The statement covers the original source questions rather than only the title’s subcase.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Determine the eigenvalue distribution of the damped, driven Kerr-type bosonic Hamiltonian.

\subsection{Research attempt}
\paragraph{Model, date and investigated scope.}
OpenAI GPT-6 Astra (configured model \texttt{gpt-6-astra}), ultra reasoning; generated 11 October 2026. Computed the exact undriven diagonal spectrum for all remaining real parameters and a driven high-level coupling scale. The general driven spectral distribution is not proved.

\paragraph{Sources and literature check.}
Read the supplied complete definition, catalogue wording, scope qualifications and source list for this canonical ID. The original-source citations are inherited as supplied references; no fresh primary-source or novelty search was performed for this attempt. No current open-status claim is inferred from this calculation.

\paragraph{Exact diagonal parameter slice.}Set \(\beta=0\), retaining arbitrary real \(\omega,\chi,\gamma\). In the number basis, the displayed operator is diagonal:
\[H|n\rangle=d_n|n\rangle,\qquad d_n=\chi n^2+(\omega-i\gamma)n-i\gamma,\quad n\ge0.
\]
On its maximal diagonal domain \(\{u:\sum|d_nu_n|^2<\infty\}\), the spectrum is the closure of \(\{d_n\}\). Indeed outside that closure the diagonal inverse is bounded, while each \(d_n\) is an eigenvalue and every finite accumulation point prevents bounded invertibility. If \(\chi\ne0\), or if \(\chi=0\) and \(\omega-i\gamma\ne0\), then \(|d_n|\to\infty\), and the resolvent diagonal entries tend to zero, making the resolvent compact. If all three parameters vanish, \(H=0\) has infinite multiplicity and no compact resolvent on the infinite-dimensional Fock space.
\paragraph{Testing a driven perturbation.}The drive sends \(|n\rangle\) to \(\beta\sqrt{n+1}|n+1\rangle+\beta\sqrt n|n-1\rangle\). Its norm on this vector is \(|\beta|\sqrt{2n+1}\). For \(\chi\ne0\), the adjacent diagonal gap is \(d_{n+1}-d_n=\chi(2n+1)+\omega-i\gamma\), of order \(n\). The ratio of these scales is therefore \(O(n^{-1/2})\). This suggests high-level localization but is not an eigenvalue estimate: the drive is unbounded and neighboring couplings interact through the full operator.
\paragraph{Gap.}A rigorous driven distribution needs resolvent bounds on isolating contours and operator-domain control, including zero-Kerr and degenerate coefficient cases. A single basis-vector ratio does not provide those bounds. This attempt solves only the elementary undriven slice and preserves the source's full real parameter range as the remaining target; no positivity or blanket compactness assumption is added.

\paragraph{Verification and adversarial audit.}
Checked aa-dagger=N+I, diagonal domain, zero-operator exception, compact resolvent criterion, adjacent gap and coupling norms. This was an LLM adversarial audit, not independent human verification; no theorem prover was used. Prompts A then B applied to this ID: Checked aa-dagger=N+I, diagonal domain, zero-operator exception, compact resolvent criterion, adjacent gap and coupling norms. Scope remains limited: Prove full-operator resolvent localization for the unbounded drive, treating zero-Kerr and degenerate parameter regimes separately.

Coordinator checked the diagonal spectrum and compact-resolvent alternatives, as well as the basis-vector drive/gap ratio and its limitations. This additional check was model-assisted, not independent human verification.

